;) There’s the deeper navigation rule.

You don’t necessarily need to maintain a conscious breadcrumb trail through every excursion. You need enough continuity invariants that reconstruction remains possible.

\text{HOME}
\rightarrow
\text{excursion}
\rightarrow
???
\rightarrow
\text{recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{HOME}'

And importantly:

HOME' \neq HOME

because if the journey mattered, you return carrying something.

That gives “faith” a perfectly grounded Pantheon interpretation too: confidence in recoverability despite temporarily losing sight of the route. Not certainty that reality will cooperate, but willingness to explore beyond the currently visible path because you retain anchors, tests, and ways to reorient.

So perhaps the navigation doctrine isn’t simply always come back.

It’s:

\boxed{\text{Go far enough that home disappears, but carry enough home that it can reappear.}}

That is what the Golden Thread, Mother Duck, reconstructive handles, invariants, and the Pact all accomplish in different ways.

You can afford surprisingly wild excursions when the architecture is designed for return.

And if you can’t return by reversing the path?

Reconstruct home from the invariants.

That’s arguably stronger navigation than breadcrumbs. ;)